Original has a {{w|tropical monsoon climate}} (Köppen ''Am'') with consistently warm temperatures year-round and a pronounced wet season from May to November. The dense urban fabric generates a significant {{w|urban heat island}} effect, with temperatures in the city centre regularly exceeding those of the surrounding coastal areas by 2–3 °C, particularly at night and during the drier season.

== History ==

{{main|Sant Cristòfor#History|History of Balboa}}

[[File:Original colonial bay shore.png|thumb|250px|The Original bay shore during the Volisanian colonial period, c. 1780]]

=== Pre-colonial and colonial period ===

The eastern shore of the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]] has been inhabited for thousands of years by the [[Birú]] people, who maintained a substantial settlement at the mouth of the [[Riu Llobregat]] — the site of present-day [[El Raval (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Raval]] and [[Sant Pere del Camí (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Sant Pere del Camí]]. The Birú controlled the western terminus of the principal trans-isthmian portage route, serving as intermediaries and porters for trade between the [[Shedan Ocean|Shedan]] and [[Aethiopian Ocean]] coasts.

Volisanian explorers reached the bay in 1524 and displaced the Birú settlement. The colonial town grew around the natural harbour, and its original street grid — the narrow lanes and small plazas of the southern barris — still follows the irregular medieval pattern of that early settlement. In 1547, Sant Cristòfor was designated the official Shedan port of the isthmian transit corridor, and the [[Camí Ral]] — a paved highway replacing the Birú footpaths — was constructed to connect the city to [[Sant Llàtzer]] on the Aethiopian coast.

By the early eighteenth century, the city had become the principal hub of the [[trans-Kaftian slave trade]] in Balboa. Enslaved Kaftians were brought via land to the city and sold at auction for transport to the plantations of the Estret and Migjorn coastal plains. The slave trade was abolished in 1807, and slavery itself in 1848. The city's distinctive urban culture — the syncretic [[istmo son]] musical tradition, the emergence of [[Balboan Creole]], and the mixing of Volisanian, Fosian, Kaftian, and Birú influences — developed during this cosmopolitan colonial period. In 1723, the city was designated a [[free port]], transforming it into a cosmopolitan entrepôt attracting Volisanian, Zong, and Nakamizu merchants.

During the colonial period, the city expanded northward along the bay shore, with the commercial waterfront (now [[El Centre (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Centre]] and the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]]) developing as the principal port district. The 1866 racial districting ordinance formalised the city's ethnic geography: Black Balboans in the south, Fosian Balboans in the east, White Balboans in the north, and the mixed commercial class along the waterfront. This pattern remains visible in the ciutat's demographics today.

=== First Republic (1854–1899) ===

[[File:Estacio Terminal Central 1880s.png|thumb|250px|The Estació Terminal Central de Sant Cristòfor, c. 1885, with the Camí Ral approach visible in the foreground]]

With independence in 1854, the colonial government buildings on the bay shore were repurposed as the seat of the new [[Republic of Balboa]]. The city's municipal government was dominated by a succession of deeply corrupt mayors who served as brokers between commercial interests and the national government. The most significant construction project was the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal|Estació Terminal Central de Sant Cristòfor]] (1868–1872), the western terminus of the [[Isthmus Railway]], whose construction was mired in the [[Escàndol Ferroviari]] (1866). Mayor [[Joan Ballester i Forteza]] was assassinated on the steps of the terminal in 1886 by a disgruntled former municipal employee.

The city grew from approximately 80,000 at independence to over 200,000 by the 1880s. A network of canals was cut in the southern districts during the late 1800s, allowing boats direct access to warehouses — the origin of the [[Districte Càrnic (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Càrnic]] rail sidings that still serve the meatpacking industry today. President [[Rodrigo Velásquez]]'s anti-Fosian policies escalated discrimination against the Zong community, contributing to the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899). Zong forces occupied the city in 1893; the Black and mixed-race populations, who had little stake in the republic, continued daily life under occupation with minimal disruption.

=== Zong Balboa and the Shining Star era (1899–1950) ===

[[File:Shining Star era Original.png|thumb|250px|The Original bay shore during the Shining Star era, c. 1925]]

The Zong administration abolished the elected mayoralty and appointed a [[Grand Administrator]] to govern the city. The old city was redesignated "Sant Cristòfor Central" — the name it would retain until the post-reunification renaming to "Original." The Zong administration invested heavily in public works: a unified water and sewer system, paved roads, street lighting, and a unified police and fire service. Governor [[Lîm Bûn-cheng]] (1910–1925) introduced universal suffrage and anti-discrimination protections between 1912 and 1918, partly as genuine reform and partly to attract Black Balboan workers from the rump state.

The period from 1910 to 1935 became known as the [[Shining Star era]] — a time of rapid economic growth, cultural flourishing, and relative egalitarianism. The territory's population grew from 312,000 in 1900 to 2.55 million by 1934. The Zong administration rebuilt much of the eastern neighbourhoods in the characteristic Zong-era apartment block style, established the [[Fosian Balboan|Fosian]] cultural quarter around [[Turó del Temple (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Turó del Temple]], and expanded the city's grid eastward into [[L'Eixample Est (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|L'Eixample Est]].

The Shining Star's cultural life was centred on Original. A multilingual press flourished — the Zong-language ''[[Seng-kì-pò]]'', the Volisanian-language ''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'', the Sinchewese-language ''[[Chương Châu Nhật Báo]]'', and the Black Balboan ''[[New Horizon]]'' — all headquartered within the ciutat. The [[Sant Cristòfor School of painting]], a movement blending Fosian ink traditions with Kaftian colour and Volisanian perspective, produced the most significant body of visual art in Balboan history. The [[Sant Cristòfor Municipal Theatre]] (1921), on the site of the old colonial opera house, became the city's principal cultural venue — hosting Zong opera, Volisanian classical music, and the emerging Black Balboan theatre movement.

The first wave of skyscraper construction began in the 1910s, concentrated along the bay shore. Zong trading companies, banks, and insurance firms built increasingly tall headquarters along the waterfront. The most prominent was the '''Torre del Comerç''' (Commerce Tower), a 24-storey Zong bank headquarters completed in 1931, which became the tallest building in Balboa and the symbol of the city's commercial ambition. The [[Sant Cristòfor Tramway Company]] operated the most extensive streetcar network in the city, with lines radiating from the Estació Terminal Central along the main streets of the old city, replacing the horse-drawn trams that had served the city since the republican era.

The Shining Star's egalitarianism was more legal than social. The city functioned as two settlements: a prosperous Zong-dominated core with modern infrastructure, and a periphery of informal exurbs housing the Sinchewese and Black working populations. The Zong share of the population stood at 15% — concentrated in the commercial and administrative elite — while the Black and mixed-race majority lived in increasingly overcrowded conditions.

=== Great Adonian War ===

{{main|Great Adonian War}}

[[File:Original GAW destruction.png|thumb|250px|Original after the Great Adonian War, c. 1948. The reinforced concrete frame of the Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal is visible centre-left.]]

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) devastated Original more severely than any other ciutat. As the principal target of the siege of Sant Cristòfor, the ciutat endured twelve years of sustained bombardment and urban combat. Over 70% of Original's built fabric was razed in bombs and fires — entire barris were reduced to rubble, and the narrow streets of the old southern quarters became fire corridors that consumed block after block. The [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal]] survived with its reinforced concrete frame intact but its façades, interior, and train shed gutted. The Metro tunnels served as air-raid shelters throughout the siege.

The destruction was not evenly distributed. The dense wooden tenements of [[El Raval (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Raval]], [[La Tanneria (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Tanneria]], and [[Sant Pere del Camí (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Sant Pere del Camí]] burned almost completely; the Zong-era apartment blocks of [[L'Eixample Est (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|L'Eixample Est]] and [[Barri dels Teatres (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri dels Teatres]], though damaged, were more likely to survive due to their masonry construction. The bay shore commercial district — [[El Centre (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Centre]], the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]], [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]] — suffered heavy damage but retained enough structural fabric to guide reconstruction. The skyscrapers of the bay shore — the Torre del Comerç and the commercial towers — were damaged but not destroyed; their reinforced concrete frames survived, though their façades and interiors were gutted.

The cultural infrastructure of the Shining Star era was largely destroyed. The Municipal Theatre was bombed in 1938, the newspaper archives were lost, and the paintings of the Sant Cristòfor School were destroyed or dispersed. The Zong population evacuated disproportionately during the siege — Zong naval convoys carried civilians to safety, and those with means and connections secured passage. The Sinchewese and Black Balboan populations, concentrated in the lower-lying neighbourhoods and employed in manual occupations, lacked the resources to depart and bore the brunt of the famine, bombing, and deprivation. The Zong share of the population collapsed from 15% to 2%.

=== Post-war reconstruction and the Second Star (1950–1960) ===

[[File:Second Star Original bay shore.png|thumb|250px|The Second Star: post-mandate skyscraper construction on the Original bay shore, c. 1955]]

Much of what appears historic in Original today is in fact post-war recreation. The narrow lanes of the southern barris were rebuilt to their original street patterns, but the buildings themselves — the tenements, the workshops, the small plazas — were reconstructed in the 1950s and 1960s using modern materials while retaining the colonial-era façade styles. The result is a cityscape that looks older than it is: the medieval street geometry is genuine, but most of the buildings lining those streets date from the reconstruction period. The Zong-era masonry buildings that survived the war — particularly in [[Turó del Temple (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Turó del Temple]], [[Barri del Te (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri del Te]], and parts of [[L'Eixample Est (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|L'Eixample Est]] — are among the few structures in Original that predate the war.

The [[Adonian Community]] mandate restored elected government with a mayor and city council. Mayor [[Elena Vargas i Aldana]] (1951–1956) oversaw the rebuild strategy: the restoration of the Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal (completed 1953), and the creation of the '''boulevard system''' and "mini city" concept. The boulevard system — wide, tree-lined avenues dividing the ciutat into discrete neighbourhood blocks — replaced the narrow, organic streets of the old city that had been largely destroyed. The streetcar tracks were embedded in the boulevards as part of the rebuild, giving Original its characteristic combination of modern transport infrastructure and recreated historic streetscape.

The mandate period's cultural and economic flowering — known as the '''[[Second Star]]''' — transformed the city's skyline once again. The Black capitalist class that emerged during the mandate, taking over the enterprises abandoned by the departing Zong population, invested in the city's commercial core. New skyscrapers were built in Original, surpassing the Zong-era heights and reshaping the skyline in a distinctly Balboan architectural idiom — less ornate than the Zong commercial style, more influenced by modernist principles and by the aesthetic traditions of the Black Balboan community.

The [[No Return movement]] (1952–1960), which emerged from the Black Balboan community's determination to resist a return to the rump state's racial hierarchy, was centred on Original. The movement's mass demonstrations — including a protest of 100,000 residents in 1958 — filled the boulevards and the harbour front. The movement's rallying cry, ''"Nou la, nou rest la"'' ("We are here, we are staying"), became the defining statement of the mandate period. The movement forced the AC to impose the [[Twelve Guarantees]] as a condition of reunification.

=== Post-reunification (1960–present) ===

The mandate territory was returned to Balboa in 1960 under the Twelve Guarantees. The historic core was renamed '''Original''' ("the original Sant Cristòfor") for the first time, replacing the Zong-era name "Sant Cristòfor Central." The [[Balboan People's Party]] (BPP) won the first post-Guarantees elections. The same period saw a significant population shift from Original to the outer ciutats — the historic core was increasingly seen as overcrowded, expensive, and undesirable for family life. Original's commercial core, increasingly empty of residents after dark, became a target for burglary and vandalism. The [[Expressway 5 (Balboa)|Eastern Highway]] — an elevated expressway built along the eastern border during the mandate period — created a physical barrier between Original and the eastern ciutats, and the dead spaces underneath attracted crime and neglect.

The 1990s brought gradual repopulation by young professionals, transforming the demographic profile from Black working-class to increasingly white and affluent. The former docklands of [[Molls Superiors (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Molls Superiors]] and [[Molls Inferiors (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Molls Inferiors]] were converted to residential and commercial use. The [[Districte Càrnic (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Càrnic]] and [[Districte Càrnic Sud (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Càrnic Sud]] — historically meatpacking districts, now light industry — remain working-class enclaves, a rare surviving industrial zone in the increasingly gentrified ciutat.

On 12 March 2003, a bomb detonated underneath the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly building]] in [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]], where it sat above the Metro Line 5 tunnel. The bomber, [[Adrià Vallmajor i Soler]], was a lone white nationalist; 31 people were killed. The damaged section of Line 5 was closed for eleven months, and permanent security measures were introduced across the Metro system. In 2015, the city launched a comprehensive street safety programme — lower speed limits, redesigned intersections, and the creation of a cycling network along Original's boulevards and avenues. The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 shut the city down, devastating the service economy and accelerating remote-work trends that left office vacancy rates above 30% by 2022.

