== Transport ==

{{main|Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Transport in Sant Cristòfor}}

[[File:Original transport map.png|thumb|250px|Transport map of Original showing metro, streetcar, and ferry routes]]

Original is the transport hub of Sant Cristòfor. Seven of the city's nine metro lines converge in the ciutat, and the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal|Estació Terminal Central]] at [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]] is the western terminus of the [[Isthmus Railway]].

=== Metro ===

{{main|Metro de Sant Cristòfor}}

Seven of the city's nine metro lines serve Original (Lines 1–7). Line 8 clips the southeast corner only; Line 9 does not serve the ciutat. There are approximately 50 stations in Original, including several major interchange hubs.

The principal metro hubs in Original are:

* '''[[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]]''' — 4 lines (L3, L4, L5, L7); the city's busiest interchange
* '''[[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]]''' — 3 lines (L3, L5, L7); adjacent to the Estació Terminal Central
* '''[[Estació Terminal Central]]''' — Lines 1 and 2; the Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal, western terminus of the Isthmus Railway
* '''[[La Diagonal (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Diagonal]]''' — 3 lines (L3, L5, L7); luxury shopping and museum district
* '''[[Molls Superiors (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Molls Superiors]]''' — 3 lines (L3, L5, L7); waterfront interchange
* '''[[L'Eixample Sud (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|L'Eixample Sud]]''' — 3 lines (L3, L4, L7); southern interchange

Three barris — [[El Centre (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Centre]], [[La Sagrada (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Sagrada]], and [[Les Vil·les (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Les Vil·les]] — have no metro station and are served by the streetcar and bus networks.

=== Streetcar ===

{{main|Tramvia de Sant Cristòfor}}

Eight streetcar lines run along the main boulevards of Original, providing last-mile connections where metro stations are too far apart. The boulevard system — wide, tree-lined avenues that divide the ciutat into discrete neighbourhood blocks — was laid out during the post-GAW reconstruction, replacing the narrow, organic streets of the old city that had been largely destroyed. The streetcar tracks were embedded in the boulevards as part of the rebuild, giving Original its characteristic combination of modern transport infrastructure and recreated historic streetscape. The principal boulevards are the '''Embarcadero''' (the waterfront boulevard along the bay shore), the '''Gran Avinguda''' (the main east–west spine), and seven diagonal avenues — '''Avinguda Llibertat''', '''Avinguda Campions''', '''Avinguda Herois''', '''Avinguda Justícia''', '''Avinguda Responsabilitat''', '''Avinguda Fe''', and '''Avinguda Solidaritat''' — which cross the ciutat from the Estació Terminal Central through the neighbourhood blocks.

* '''T1''': Estació Terminal Central → Embarcadero → financial district → southern neighbourhoods
* '''T2''': Estació Terminal Central → Gran Avinguda → Dàrsena border
* '''T3''': Estació Terminal Central → Avinguda Llibertat → Costa Nord border
* '''T4''': Estació Terminal Central → Avinguda Campions → Sant Jordi border
* '''T5–T8''': Connector lines along the remaining diagonal avenues, linking the boulevard spines to intermediate neighbourhoods, metro stations, and the ferry terminal

=== Bus ===

{{main|Bus de Sant Cristòfor}}

The bus network in Original is a feeder system, complementing the metro and streetcar rather than duplicating their routes. Bus routes originate within the neighbourhood blocks and carry passengers to the nearest metro station or boulevard, where they transfer to the metro or streetcar for longer-distance travel. The diagonal layout of the avenues leaves triangular gaps between them — the interior of the "mini city" blocks — and the bus routes fill these gaps, serving the narrow streets and residential areas that are too far from a metro entrance or a boulevard stop.

The bus service operates 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. During the day, buses follow their feeder routes within the neighbourhood blocks. At night, when the metro and streetcar close, the buses take over the streetcar routes along the boulevards, providing the only public transport along the main avenues. Night service runs at reduced frequency but covers the same diagonal corridors as the streetcar lines T1–T8. The bus network is particularly important for the three barris without metro service — [[El Centre (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Centre]], [[La Sagrada (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Sagrada]], and [[Les Vil·les (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Les Vil·les]] — where the bus is the primary connection to the wider transport network.

=== Ferry ===

{{main|Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor}}

The [[Sant Cristòfor Main ferry terminal]] in [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]] is the principal mainland ferry terminal, providing connections to [[Pandao]] (Barcelona), [[Illa del Portal]], [[Costa Nord]], and [[Daurada]].

=== Rail ===

The [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal|Estació Terminal Central de Sant Cristòfor]] at [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]] is the western terminus of the [[Isthmus Railway]] and the principal long-distance and commuter rail station in Sant Cristòfor.

==== Commuter rail ====

Six commuter rail lines converge at the Estació Terminal Central, connecting Original to the outer ciutats and suburbs. The commuter rail service, branded [[CristoCarril]], is operated by [[Intercon]]:

* [[Line B (CristoCarril)|Line B]] (Original–Mare de Déu la Mar) — serves [[La Garriga (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Garriga]] and [[Sant Antoni (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Sant Antoni]] within Original
* [[Line C (CristoCarril)|Line C]] (Original–Sant Llorenç)
* [[Line D (CristoCarril)|Line D]] (Original–L'Estació) — serves [[La Garriga (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Garriga]] and [[Sant Antoni (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Sant Antoni]] within Original; continues to [[Ciutat de l'Estació]] and the airport
* [[Line E (CristoCarril)|Line E]] (Original–Sant Martí)
* [[Line F (CristoCarril)|Line F]] (Original–Sant Jordi)
* [[Line G (CristoCarril)|Line G]] (Original–Costa Nord)

==== Long-distance and high-speed rail ====

The Estació Terminal Central is also the terminus for long-distance and high-speed rail services connecting Sant Cristòfor to the rest of Balboa and beyond:

* [[Sant Cristòfor–Portnou Line]] — high-speed rail to [[Portnou]] and the [[Balboa Canal]]
* [[Sant Cristòfor–Areza Line]] — high-speed rail to the [[International City of Areza]]
* [[Sant Cristòfor–Sant Bart Line]] — [[Sant Bart]]
* [[Sant Cristòfor–Haicang Line]] — [[Haicang]]
* [[Sant Cristòfor–Bellver Line]] — [[Bellver]]
* [[Sant Cristòfor–Sant Llàtzer Line]] — [[Sant Llàtzer]] (Aethiopian coast terminus of the Isthmus Railway)
* [[Sant Cristòfor–Campdària Line]] — [[Campdària]]
* [[Sant Cristòfor–Portblanc Line]] — [[Portblanc]]

== Economy ==

[[File:Districte Financer Original.png|thumb|250px|The Districte Financer skyline at dusk]]

=== Financial centre ===

Original is the financial and governmental centre of Balboa. The [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] houses the [[Borsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (the national stock exchange), the headquarters of most major Balboan banks, and the regional offices of international firms. The district's skyline — a layered record of the city's economic history — includes Zong-era commercial towers (1910s–1930s), Second Star skyscrapers built by the post-war Black capitalist class (1950s–1960s), and the glass-and-steel towers of the global financial era (2000s–present). The [[Torre del Comerç]], the 24-storey Zong bank headquarters completed in 1931, survived the Great Adonian War and remains a landmark, though it has long since been surpassed by newer towers.

The financial sector's growth has been driven by the [[Balboa Canal]] and the logistics, insurance, and legal services that have grown around it. The [[International City of Areza]] and the Adonian Community institutions based there generate additional demand for banking and professional services. The Borsa de Sant Cristòfor, established during the Shining Star era, is the principal securities exchange in Lurandia.

=== Government sector ===

The [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]] sits in [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]], and most government ministries have their headquarters in the waterfront barris. The government sector is a major employer — the national civil service, the diplomatic corps (most foreign embassies are located in or near Original), and the Adonian Community liaison offices all contribute to the ciutat's employment base. The concentration of government institutions in Original has been a source of political tension, with successive governments proposing to decentralise functions to [[Sant Bart]] or other cities, but the proposals have never been implemented.

=== Commerce and trade ===

Original's commercial history stretches back to its founding as a free port in 1723. The [[Mercat Plaça (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Mercat Plaça]] market, one of the largest traditional markets in Abyala, continues to operate on the bay shore — a direct descendant of the colonial-era market that served the trans-isthmian trade. The market's Birú traders, food stalls, and open-air commerce remain a vital part of the ciutat's commercial life, even as the surrounding district has been transformed by office towers and luxury hotels.

The [[Sant Cristòfor Main ferry terminal]] in [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]] handles passenger and cargo traffic across the bay, connecting Original to [[Pandao]] (Barcelona), [[Illa del Portal]], [[Costa Nord]], and [[Daurada]]. The ferry terminal, the Estació Terminal Central, and the metro interchange at Plaça Major make Original the principal transport and logistics hub of the isthmian corridor.

=== Industry ===

The [[Districte Càrnic (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Càrnic]] and [[Districte Càrnic Sud (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Càrnic Sud]] retain their historic names — "meatpacking districts" — but the meatpacking industry has largely departed. The districts are now home to light industry, small manufacturing, food processing, and logistics operations, served by the canal-side rail sidings and the commuter rail network. The rail infrastructure and the established industrial character have so far prevented conversion to residential use, though gentrification pressure continues to mount. The districts remain one of the few sources of blue-collar employment in Original and a working-class enclave in an increasingly affluent ciutat.

The former docklands of [[Molls Superiors (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Molls Superiors]] and [[Molls Inferiors (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Molls Inferiors]] were converted to residential and commercial use in the 1990s, part of the broader urban regeneration that transformed the waterfront. The canal network in the southern barris, originally built for warehouse access during the First Republic, has been cleaned and landscaped, transforming the industrial waterways into a waterfront amenity.

[[El Forn de Baix (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Forn de Baix]] — the "fashion district" — is the ciutat's most visible example of industrial conversion. The barri's textile factories, which once supplied the Zong-era garment trade, have been occupied by streetwear labels, underground designers, and independent fashion retailers. The district's industrial architecture — high ceilings, exposed brick, large windows — provides the raw, unfinished aesthetic that the fashion community favours, and the concentration of designers, manufacturers, and retailers in a single neighbourhood has created a self-reinforcing creative cluster.

=== Tourism and hospitality ===

Original's cultural institutions, historic barris, and bay shore location make it the principal tourist destination in Balboa. The museum strip along [[La Diagonal (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Diagonal]], the dining halls of [[Barri dels Teatres (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri dels Teatres]], the teahouses of [[Barri del Te (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri del Te]], and the annual [[Festival del Son Istme]] draw visitors from across Balboa and internationally.

The '''[[Sant Cristòfor Convention Centre]]''', located in the plaça north of the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal|Estació Terminal Central]], is the largest convention facility in Balboa. The centre hosts trade fairs, international conferences, and corporate events, and is a major driver of business tourism to the ciutat. Its location adjacent to the terminal — with direct connections to six commuter rail lines, eight long-distance rail services, and three metro lines — makes it one of the most accessible convention venues in Lurandia.

The hotel sector is concentrated along the bay shore and in the historic core, with properties ranging from international luxury chains in the Districte Financer to boutique hotels in converted Zong-era shophouses and colonial mansions. The hospitality sector is a significant employer, though the [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 devastated the industry — occupancy rates collapsed, and several hotels closed permanently.

Short-term rentals have been a persistent political issue. The rapid growth of platform-based short-term letting in the 2010s — driven by the same gentrification that repopulated Original — removed residential units from the long-term housing market and contributed to the affordability crisis. The Original ciutat council banned short-term rentals in most neighbourhoods, citing the loss of housing stock and the disruption of residential communities by transient visitors. The regulations vary by barri: some impose outright bans on rentals shorter than 30 days, others permit them only in designated zones or require annual licensing with strict caps. The enforcement is uneven, and illegal short-term letting remains a source of tension between residents, landlords, and the ciutat council.

=== Gentrification and housing ===

The ciutat's economic transformation has come at a social cost. The 1990s repopulation of Original by young professionals transformed the demographic profile from Black working-class to increasingly white and affluent. Property values in the restored barris rose sharply, displacing long-term residents and the community institutions — mutual aid societies, religious brotherhoods, informal markets — that had sustained the Black community for generations. The [[Expressway 5 (Balboa)|Eastern Highway]], which created a physical barrier between Original and the eastern ciutats, exacerbated the inequality: the neighbourhoods immediately to its east, predominantly working-class and Black, suffered from noise, pollution, and the loss of connectivity to the city centre.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 devastated the service economy — thousands of workers in hospitality, retail, and personal services lost their jobs, and the small businesses that lined the boulevards were forced to close, many permanently. The shift to remote work left office vacancy rates above 30% by 2022, raising questions about the future of the financial district and the commercial core. Mayor [[Oriol Cardona i Ousmane]] (FSB), elected in 2025, has pledged to expand social housing and strengthen tenants' protections as part of a broader response to the affordability crisis.

== Education ==

[[File:Universitat de Sant Cristofor Original.png|thumb|250px|The Universitat de Sant Cristòfor campus in La Universitat]]

=== Universities ===

Original is home to five universities and the Sant Cristòfor City College:

* The '''[[Universitat de Sant Cristòfor]]''' in [[La Universitat (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Universitat]] — the largest university in Balboa, founded in 1871. The university was established during the First Republic as a colonial-style institution serving the Volisanian elite, but was opened to Black and mixed-race students during the Zong period under the anti-discrimination reforms. The campus occupies a dense urban site in the La Universitat barri, mixing Volisanian colonial cloisters, Zong-era academic buildings, and 1960s concrete modernism. The university is the principal centre of higher education in Sant Cristòfor, with faculties in law, medicine, engineering, humanities, and the sciences.

* The '''[[Balboan Jesuit University]]''' in [[La Seu (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Seu]] — a private Catholic university, founded in 1903. The Jesuit university was established by the religious order that had maintained a presence in Sant Cristòfor since the colonial period. It is smaller and more selective than the Universitat de Sant Cristòfor, with strengths in philosophy, theology, law, and the liberal arts. The campus is adjacent to the Jesuit church and monastery that give La Seu its name ("the See").

* The '''[[Universitat de l'Istme]]''' in [[L'Eixample Est (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|L'Eixample Est]] — a public university founded in 1964 during the post-reunification expansion of higher education. The Universitat de l'Istme was established to meet the growing demand for university places from the working-class and immigrant communities who were less likely to attend the more elite Universitat de Sant Cristòfor. It has strengths in education, social work, public health, and urban planning.

* The '''[[Zong University of Sant Cristòfor]]''' in [[Barri del Te (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri del Te]] — a private university originally founded by the Zong community in 1912 as a Zong-language institution. After the GAW and the collapse of the Zong population, the university was reconstituted as a multilingual institution under the Twelve Guarantees' cultural protections. It retains Zong and Sinchewese language programmes and is the principal centre for Fosian studies in Balboa.

* The '''[[Universitat Tècnica de Sant Cristòfor]]''' in [[Districte Càrnic (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Càrnic]] — a public technical university, founded in 1971, specialising in engineering, applied sciences, and industrial technology. The university was established to support the industrial workforce and has close ties to the light industry and logistics sectors in the Districte Càrnic area.

=== Sant Cristòfor City College ===

The '''[[Sant Cristòfor City College]]''' (Col·legi Municipal de Sant Cristòfor) is a public two-year college system with six campuses across Original, providing vocational training, associate degrees, and continuing education. The City College was established in 1968 as part of the post-reunification expansion of access to higher education, and it serves a predominantly working-class and immigrant student body. The six campuses are distributed across the ciutat, one in each of the major neighbourhood clusters:

* '''Plaça Major campus''' — business and finance programmes
* '''El Raval campus''' — health sciences and nursing
* '''Turó del Temple campus''' — Fosian languages and cultural studies
* '''La Verneda campus''' — engineering technology and trades
* '''Sant Gervasi campus''' — education and teacher training
* '''Mercat Plaça campus''' — hospitality, tourism, and culinary arts

=== Other institutions ===

* The '''[[Sant Cristòfor Academy]]''' — a secondary school founded during the colonial period, originally for the education of the Volisanian elite. The Academy became the principal pathway for Black Balboan advancement during the Shining Star era, producing the doctors, educators, merchants, and professionals who formed the Black middle class. Graduates of the Academy were central to the Pandao community and the No Return movement. The Academy continues to operate as a prestigious secondary school.

* The '''[[Conservatori de Sant Cristòfor]]''' — a music conservatory in [[Barri dels Teatres (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri dels Teatres]], founded in 1958 during the Second Star. The conservatory was instrumental in the formalisation of [[istmo son]] as a genre and in the training of the musicians who defined the genre's early period.

* The '''[[Sant Cristòfor School of Art]]''' — a visual arts school in [[La Diagonal (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Diagonal]], successor to the traditions of the Sant Cristòfor School of painting. The school was rebuilt after the GAW and continues to produce artists working in the syncretic tradition that blends Fosian, Kaftian, and Volisanian visual culture.

=== Primary and secondary schools ===

The boulevard system's "mini city" concept was designed so that each neighbourhood block would contain its own schools, along with shops, community facilities, and public spaces. As a result, Original has a dense network of primary and secondary schools distributed across the 31 barris, rather than concentrated in a single educational district. The schools are operated by the municipal education authority, with some community-run schools providing Zong and Sinchewese language instruction under the Twelve Guarantees' cultural protections.

The Zong administration invested heavily in schools during the Shining Star era, building modern school buildings and funding teachers' salaries. The Black community's mutual aid societies had previously provided the only schooling available to Black children; the Zong-funded schools replaced much of this informal provision, though the community-run schools persisted alongside them. The post-war reconstruction rebuilt the damaged school stock, and the "mini city" concept ensured that every rebuilt neighbourhood block included school facilities.

== Culture ==

[[File:Original cultural quarters.png|thumb|250px|The cultural quarters of Original, viewed from the bay]]

Original is the cultural heart of Balboa. The ciutat's cultural life has been shaped by successive waves of Volisanian, Fosian, Kaftian, and Birú influence, producing a distinctive syncretic culture that is neither wholly Illypnian nor wholly Adonian. The layered history of the city — colonial port, slave trade hub, Zong commercial centre, post-war Black Balboan capital — is visible in its cultural institutions, its neighbourhoods, and its daily life.

=== Istmo son ===

{{main|Istmo son}}

[[File:Istmo son performance Original.png|thumb|250px|An istmo son ''trobada'' (gathering) in a Barri Negre courtyard, c. 1960]]

Original is the birthplace of [[istmo son]], the most distinctive musical genre in Balboa. The genre emerged in the 1950s in the '''Barri Negre''' — the historic Black quarter within [[El Raval (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Raval]] — where small acoustic ensembles began playing in courtyard ''trobades'' (gatherings) that blended Volisanian guitar and brass, Kaftian polyrhythmic percussion (congas, bongos, marímbula), and Fosian melodic sensibility. The music was sung in [[Balboan Creole]], with call-and-response vocals between a ''cantor'' and ''cor''. The genre was named by cultural correspondent Oriol Cardona in a 1958 article in ''[[La Voz del Istmo]]''.

The earliest documented ensemble was the '''Barri Negre Trio''' (1953–1962). Dionis Closa made the first commercial recordings in 1963 on [[Istmo Records]], and the annual '''Festival del Son Istme''', held in Original since 1972, became the national musical event. The genre evolved through several phases — ''son antiguo'' (1950s acoustic), ''son moderno'' (1960s brass and electric), ''son de interior'' (1970s sombre interior style), ''istmo son electrónico'' (1990s electronic production), and ''son-fusió'' (1990s Fosian jazz fusion). The [[Sant Bart Collective]]'s 1991 Creole hip hop recordings, bridging istmo son and hip hop, originated from the same cultural milieu.

=== Visual arts ===

{{main|Sant Cristòfor School of painting}}

The [[Sant Cristòfor School of painting]], active during the Shining Star era (1910–1935), was the most significant body of visual art in Balboan history. The school blended Fosian ink traditions with Kaftian colour and Volisanian perspective, producing a distinctive style that reflected the city's multi-ethnic composition. The school's works were housed in the Municipal Gallery on the bay shore — most were destroyed or dispersed during the Great Adonian War, and surviving canvases are among the most valuable artworks in Balboa.

The [[Second Star]] period (1950–1960) produced a second generation of Black Balboan visual artists, working in a modernist idiom influenced by the aesthetic traditions of the Black community. The [[Municipal Gallery]] was rebuilt in 1955 and remains the principal visual arts venue in Original.

=== Theatre and dining halls ===

[[File:Barri dels Teatres dining hall.png|thumb|250px|A dining hall in [[Barri dels Teatres (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri dels Teatres]], c. 1975]]

The [[Sant Cristòfor Municipal Theatre]] (1921), on the site of the old colonial opera house, was the city's principal cultural venue during the Shining Star era — hosting Zong opera, Volisanian classical music, and the emerging Black Balboan theatre movement. The theatre was bombed in 1938 and destroyed; it was rebuilt in 1955 as part of the Second Star reconstruction.

The '''dining halls''' (''sales de menjar'') of [[Barri dels Teatres (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri dels Teatres]] are a distinctive cultural institution unique to Original. Large communal eating halls that evolved from the Zong-era teahouse tradition, the dining halls serve multi-course meals at long communal tables, combining Fosian tea culture with Kaftian communal cooking and Volisanian café society. The dining halls became the social hub of the working-class barris — places to eat, argue, organise, and celebrate. They remain central to the ciutat's social life, and the lunch-hour rush from the financial district fills the halls daily.

=== Language and press ===

{{main|Balboan Creole}}

[[Balboan Creole]], the creole language that emerged from the contact between Volisanian, Kaftian languages, and Fosian linguistic influences, was born in the port districts of Original. The Creole first developed in the port districts and the plantation camps, where enslaved Kaftians, Volisanian overseers, and Zong traders needed a common tongue. By the late colonial period, Balboan Creole was the vernacular of the city's working-class and Black populations, while Volisanian remained the language of government, commerce, and the colonial elite. Today, Creole is the first language of the majority of Original's Black and mixed-race residents, and code-switching between Volisanian and Creole is common across all ethnic groups.

Original was the centre of the multilingual press that flourished during the Shining Star era. The Zong-language ''[[Seng-kì-pò]]'', the Volisanian-language ''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'', the Sinchewese-language ''[[Chương Châu Nhật Báo]]'', and the Black Balboan ''[[New Horizon]]'' were all headquartered within the ciutat. The newspaper archives were lost during the Great Adonian War, but the tradition of multilingual journalism continued in the post-war period with ''[[La Voz del Istmo]]'', the voice of the No Return movement and the Black professional class.

=== Cultural quarters ===

Original's cultural life is concentrated in several distinct quarters:

* The '''theatre district''' in [[Barri dels Teatres (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri dels Teatres]] — the city's principal entertainment zone, with large dining halls, theatres, and Zong/Sinchewse cultural venues
* The '''tea quarter''' in [[Barri del Te (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri del Te]] — famous for its traditional teahouses, Zong galleries, publishing houses, and media studios
* The '''Nakamian cultural heart''' in [[Petit Nakamizu (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Petit Nakamizu]] — Kairyū Buddhist temples, Nakamian shops, ''balboazushi'' restaurants, and the annual Nakamian Cultural Parade
* The '''market culture''' of [[Mercat Plaça (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Mercat Plaça]] — the city's principal traditional market, with Birú traders, food stalls, and open-air commerce
* The '''museum strip''' along [[La Diagonal (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Diagonal]] — high-end museums, galleries, and cultural institutions on the bay waterfront
* The '''Barri Negre''' within [[El Raval (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|El Raval]] — the historic Black quarter, birthplace of istmo son, and centre of the No Return movement

== Cuisine ==

{{main|Cuisine of Sant Cristòfor}}

[[File:Original cafeteria hall.png|thumb|250px|A basement cafeteria hall in the Districte Financer, lunchtime rush]]

Original is the culinary centre of Balboa. The ciutat's food culture reflects its layered history — Volisanian colonial, Kaftian, Zong, Sinchewese, and international — and its eating establishments range from basement cafeteria halls to three of the top restaurants on the [[Abyala's 50 Best Restaurants]] list.

=== Cafeteria halls ===

The cafeteria hall (''cafeteria'' or ''sala de menjars'') is a distinctive Original institution. These are basement-level food halls, typically located beneath office buildings and commercial premises in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] and the bay shore commercial district, where multiple independent vendors operate stalls within a single shared space. The model emerged in the early twentieth century to serve the working-class population of the bay shore — dockworkers, labourers, and market traders — providing affordable, substantial meals. Each vendor specialises in a single dish or a small repertoire — one stall serves [[olloc]], another [[arros de la Badia]], another [[escudella negra]] — and the hall provides shared seating, a communal atmosphere, and prices that remain well below those of the restaurants above ground.

As Original's demographic profile shifted from working-class to professional in the 1990s and 2000s, the cafeteria halls adapted. The same stalls that once fed dockworkers now serve the financial sector's lunch crowd, and the prices have risen accordingly, though they remain the most affordable sit-down eating option in the city centre. The halls are busiest between noon and two o'clock, when the office workers descend from the towers above, and the atmosphere — crowded, noisy, aromatic — is one of the characteristic experiences of eating in Original.

=== Dining halls ===

The '''dining halls''' (''sales de menjar'') of [[Barri dels Teatres (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri dels Teatres]] are a separate tradition from the basement cafeterias. Large communal eating halls that evolved from the Zong-era teahouse tradition, the dining halls serve multi-course meals at long communal tables, combining Fosian tea culture with Kaftian communal cooking and Volisanian café society. The dining halls became the social hub of the working-class barris — places to eat, argue, organise, and celebrate. They remain central to the ciutat's social life, and the lunch-hour rush from the financial district fills the halls daily.

=== Street food ===

The [[Carrer del Canal]] district in southern Original is the city's principal street food corridor, with stalls operating from early morning through late night. The stalls serve a mix of Creole fried snacks — empanadas, tostones, chicharrones — Zong-style rice bowls, Kaftian one-pot dishes, and the noodle preparations of the Fosian community. The [[Mercat Plaça (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Mercat Plaça]] market is another major street food destination, with Birú traders selling traditional preparations alongside Creole and Fosian stalls.

=== Fine dining ===

Original is the centre of Balboan fine dining. The city has eight restaurants on the [[Abyala's 50 Best Restaurants]] list — the highest concentration of any city in Abyala — all of them located in the Original bay shore and financial district. Three restaurants hold three stars in the [[Frequent Traveller Guide]]: [[Atsui (restaurant)|Atsui]], a Sanese omakase restaurant that reached Nº1 on the Abyala list in 2015; [[DesprésDesprés]], a Black Balboan–Zong restaurant at Nº4 (2020), known for its family-style tasting menu and group-only dining policy; and [[Magpalibog]], a Dagit heritage restaurant at Nº18 (2023). The fine-dining scene is concentrated in the Districte Financer and the [[La Diagonal (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Diagonal]] museum strip, where the major restaurants occupy spaces in or adjacent to the office towers and luxury hotels.

=== Signature dishes ===

The dishes most associated with Original include:

* [[Olloc]] — a Zong-Balboan rice noodle soup with beef, invented at [[Xef Cafe]] during the Zong occupation; consumed across all social classes from street stalls to fine dining
* [[Arros de la Badia]] — a Volisanian-Kaftian rice dish analogous to paella, built on a Creole flavour base with seafood and chicken; the standard celebratory meal of the Creole-speaking community
* [[Escudella negra]] — a Black Balboan chicken braise in a dark sauce of chili, chocolate, and spices; associated with the historic southern neighbourhoods and the cafeteria halls
* [[Fideus Zong-Balboana]] — stir-fried Zong rice noodles with Balboan ingredients (shrimp, plantains, local vegetables); most commonly found in the Fosian eateries of the eastern neighbourhoods
* [[Pebre de mar]] — a Kaftian-influenced seafood stew in chili, tomato, and coconut milk; a staple of the working-class neighbourhoods and harbour markets

